reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The single draw only assigns which cyclic composition each seat receives. The compositions are equivalent up to relabelling, apart from the Red tie order. Chance accounts for roughly 10-15% of outcomes (tie order plus seat interplay).

With all players playing well, the winner is decided by:
- bid shading in sealed auctions;
- timing of the season-ending card, since the auctioneer controls which artist reaches 3;
- pushing artists you own;
- reading which opponent holds which composition. You know your own composition; the others are one of two permutations, narrowed as cards are revealed.

The draw alone does not decide the winner.

There is real inference and a way to act on it. Players deduce opponents' remaining hands from public reveals and judge opponents' card valuations from announced bids and owned counts. They also predict who will end the season, and act on all of this through bids and card choice.

A naive bidder (always 0 or always maximum) loses to a reasoning bidder, as shown under balanced, so skill is clearly favoured.
